{"seconds": 77.2, "exit": 0, "final": "Excellent! After Nxd2, White's knight attacks the e4 knight (which is doubly attacked now by the knight and the d3 pawn). And the e5 pawn is hanging to the queen. This is winning for White!\n\nSo the line is: **Qh5** and all of Black's tries lose:\n1. Qh5 Nxf2 Qxf7+ and White wins the f7 pawn with a winning attack\n2. Qh5 Bxg5 Qxf7+ Kxf7 and White is up the exchange and a pawn (won)\n3. Qh5 Bb4+ Bd2 and then Black's pieces are hanging (the knight and e5 pawn)\n\nThis is clearly the best move! It's a royal fork/double attack on f7 and h7, forcing immediate problems for Black.\n\nLet me now write the coaching note. The key lesson is: when your opponent has a knight attacking your piece, sometimes the best move isn't to defend or move the piece\u2014it's to create a forcing threat (like Qh5 attacking f7) that's more dangerous than the threat to your piece!\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Qh5\", \"line\": [\"Qh5\", \"Bb4+\", \"Bd2\", \"Bxd2+\", \"Nxd2\"], \"motifs\": [\"fork\", \"deflection\"]}\n```\n\n**Coaching Note:**\n\nWhen Black's knight attacks your bishop, resist the urge to defend it immediately. Instead, look for forcing moves that threaten the opponent's king or pawns. Here, **Qh5** creates a double attack on f7 and h7 that's too dangerous for Black to ignore. If Black tries Bb4+ Bd2, White recaptures with Nxd2 and Black's pieces fall. This teaches a key principle: **sometimes the best defense is a strong offense**\u2014create threats that matter more than the threats against you.", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "rnbqk2r/ppp1bppp/8/4p1B1/2Ppn3/1P1P4/P4PPP/RN1QKBNR w KQkq - 0 7"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "rnbqk2r/ppp1bppp/8/4p1B1/2Ppn3/1P1P4/P4PPP/RN1QKBNR w KQkq - 0 7"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "rnbqk2r/ppp1bppp/8/4p1B1/2Ppn3/1P1P4/P4PPP/RN1QKBNR w KQkq - 0 7"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_mov